"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"us","key":"google inc","loc":["United States","America, North","OECD"],"name":"Google Inc.","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Marta R.","key":"costajussàped","last":"Costa-jussà","name":"Costa-jussà, Marta R.","rank":1,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Hila","key":"gonenped","last":"Gonen","name":"Gonen, Hila","rank":2,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"},{"affno":"3","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian","key":"hardmeierped","last":"Hardmeier","locid":"9504265d-0046-4e58-b529-256a656c012d","name":"Hardmeier, Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-6103-7275","rank":3,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"},{"affno":"4","corresponding":null,"first":"Kellie","key":"websterped","last":"Webster","name":"Webster, Kellie","rank":4,"role":"Editor","rolecode":"ped"}],"pub":{"publisher":"Association for Computational Linguistics","status":"p"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-333ad955-f9fe-47b9-87a2-a57c58cb39a9"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Proceedings of the Third Workshop on Gender Bias in Natural Language Processing","type":"Book","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-382015","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
{"abstract":"Current work on coreference focuses primarily on entities, often leaving unanalysed the use ofanaphors to corefer with antecedents such as events and textual segments. Moreover, the anaphoricforms that speakers use for entity and event coreference are not mutually exclusive. This ambi-guity has been the subject of work in English, with evidence of a split between comprehenders’preferential interpretation of personal versus demonstrative pronouns. In addition, comprehendersare shown to be sensitive to antecedent complexity and aspectual status, two verb-driven cues thatsignal how an event is being portrayed. Here we extend this work via a comparison across five lan-guages (English, French, German, Italian, and Spanish). With a story-continuation experiment, wetest how different referring expressions corefer with entity and event antecedents and whether ver-bal features such as argument structure and aspect influence this choice. Our results show widelyconsistent, not categorical biases across languages: entity coreference is favoured for personal pro-nouns and event coreference for demonstratives. Antecedent complexity increases the rate at whichanaphors are taken to corefer with an event antecedent, as does portraying an event as completedthough the latter does not reach significance. Lastly, we report a comparison of the same referringexpressions to refer to entity and event antecedents in a trilingual parallel corpus annotated withcoreference. Together, the results provide a first crosslingual picture of coreference preferencesbeyond the restricted entity-only patterns targeted by most existing work on coreference. The fivelanguages are all shown to allow gradable use of pronouns for entity and event coreference, withbiases that align with existing generalizations about the link between prominence and the use ofreduced referring expressions. The studies also show the feasibility of manipulating targeted verb-driven cues across multiple languages to support crosslingual comparisons.","author_count":4,"contributors":["Bevacqua, Luca","Loáiciga, Sharid","Rohde, Hannah","Hardmeier, Christian"],"cuid":["S01-000.00-381706"],"filters":{"added":"2024-06","altsrc":["Global Data"],"author_count":"2-5 contributors","cluster_size":1,"continent":["Europe, EU","Europe, Non-EU","Nordic","OECD"],"country":["Sweden","United Kingdom"],"glorg":["All Global Affiliations","University of Edinburgh","University of Gothenburg","Uppsala University"],"keyword":["Coreference Resolution","Event anaphora","Multilingual","pronouns"],"level":"sci","mra":["sci"],"oa":"Open Access","oaiscope":"Yes","org":{"level1":["di"],"level2":["DI-ALL","DI-U"],"level3":{"U":["DI-U-ALL","DI-U-ITU"]},"level3all":["DI-U-ITU"]},"review":"pr","rtype":["clu"],"sorti":"event and entity coreference across five languages effects of context and referring expression","src":["itu"],"src-code":["DI-U-ITU"],"type":"dja","type-grp":["all","pub"]},"ft":{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5210/dad.2021.207","version":"Published Version"},"id":"S01-000.00-381706","identifier":[{"id":"85124028455","type":"scopus"}],"ids":["itu-1d7ebdb8-4ff9-4567-9cff-d8b09943ced2"],"keyword":["coreference resolution","event anaphora","multilingual","pronouns"],"lang":"English","level":"Scientific","mra":["Science/Technology"],"noi":["Global-Local-2-2-E816CE36F4C2D61ED4732D44643AA55F"],"oa_link":[{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"doi","url":"https://doi.org/10.5210/dad.2021.207","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","license":"CC BY","type":"rem","url":"https://journals.uic.edu/ojs/index.php/dad/article/view/11390","version":"Published Version"},{"access":"oa","type":"loc","url":"https://pure.itu.dk/ws/files/86429675/11390_Bevacqua_Et_Al_Event_Entity_Coreference.pdf","version":"Published Version"}],"oai":{"class":"Realised","mra":["Science / Technology (SCI)"],"yearPub":"2021","yearSub":"2021"},"org":[{"affno":"1","country":"gb","key":"university of edinburgh","loc":["United Kingdom","Europe, Non-EU","OECD"],"name":"University of Edinburgh","used":1},{"affno":"2","country":"se","key":"university of gothenburg","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"University of Gothenburg","used":1},{"affno":"3","key":"itu it university of copenhagen","l2":"DI-U","l2n":"Universities","l3":"DI-U-ITU","l3nl":"IT University of Copenhagen","l3nn":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","loc":["Denmark","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"ITU IT University of Copenhagen","namevar":["IT University of Copenhagen, Computer Science","IT University of Copenhagen, Data Science, NLPnorth"],"nora_code":"DI-U-ITU","used":1},{"affno":"4","country":"se","key":"uppsala university","loc":["Sweden","Europe, EU","Nordic","OECD"],"name":"Uppsala University","used":1}],"person":[{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Luca","key":"bevacquapau","last":"Bevacqua","name":"Bevacqua, Luca","rank":1,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"2","corresponding":null,"first":"Sharid","key":"loáicigapau","last":"Loáiciga","name":"Loáiciga, Sharid","rank":2,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"1","corresponding":null,"first":"Hannah","key":"rohdepau","last":"Rohde","name":"Rohde, Hannah","rank":3,"rolecode":"pau"},{"affno":"3 4","corresponding":null,"first":"Christian","key":"hardmeierpau","last":"Hardmeier","locid":"9504265d-0046-4e58-b529-256a656c012d","name":"Hardmeier, Christian","orcid":"0000-0002-6103-7275","rank":4,"rolecode":"pau"}],"pub":{"doi":"10.5210/dad.2021.207","issn":["21529620"],"journal":"Dialogue & Discourse","status":"p","title":"Dialogue & Discourse","title_org":"Dialogue and Discourse"},"review":"Peer Reviewed","scoID":["2-s2.0-85124028455"],"search":{"DAF":["All Danish Affiliations","All Universities","DI-ALL","DI-U","DI-U-ALL","IT University of Copenhagen","Universities"],"PST":["Published"]},"sid":["itu-1d7ebdb8-4ff9-4567-9cff-d8b09943ced2"],"src":["ITU IT University of Copenhagen"],"title":"Event and entity coreference across five languages : Effects of context and referring expression","type":"Journal Article","url":"https://local.forskningsportal.dk/search?modus=sin&s=LOI:S01-000.00-381706","year":"2021","yearsub":"2021"}
